# ⚠️ Limitations & Assumptions

ORBIT-X is an **educational spacecraft mission simulator developed using C programming**.

Its purpose is to demonstrate mission-control logic, basic aerospace concepts, and programming fundamentals—not to reproduce the complexity of real spacecraft operations.

### 1. Simplified Orbital Physics

ORBIT-X uses predefined altitude and velocity values to represent different flight stages.

It does not calculate a spacecraft's complete trajectory using gravitational forces, atmospheric drag, thrust, or numerical orbital integration.

Reaching the target altitude in the simulation does not prove that a physically valid orbit has been achieved.

### 2. Simulated Launch Requirements

The program uses simplified launch conditions:

| Parameter | Educational Requirement |
|---|---|
| Minimum Fuel | 70% |
| Maximum Payload | 20,000 kg |
| Maximum Wind Speed | 50 km/h |
| Critical Spacecraft Systems | All must pass |

These values are project assumptions and **must not be interpreted as actual NASA or aerospace launch limits**.

### 3. Simplified Fuel and Power Consumption

Fuel and battery levels decrease by predefined amounts during flight stages.

Actual spacecraft consumption depends on propulsion systems, mission duration, mass, power requirements, and operating conditions.

### 4. Manual System Diagnostics

Users manually enter whether spacecraft systems are operational.

The simulator does not communicate with real sensors, spacecraft hardware, or ground-control infrastructure.

### 5. Simplified Emergency Handling

System failures are introduced through user selections.

Safe Mode and recovery are represented through predefined programming rules rather than actual spacecraft fault-management algorithms.

### 6. No Real-Time Spacecraft Data

All telemetry values are generated within the simulator.

ORBIT-X does not connect to NASA mission systems, live satellites, or real spacecraft telemetry.

### 7. Console-Based Interface

The project operates through a text-based terminal.

It does not currently include 3D visualization, interactive orbital graphics, or a graphical mission-control dashboard.

### 8. Limited Mission Scope

The current version focuses on a simplified Earth-orbit mission.

It does not simulate interplanetary travel, spacecraft docking, atmospheric re-entry, or realistic landing operations.

---

## Why These Limitations Exist

ORBIT-X was developed as a first-semester C programming project.

The objective was to balance **scientific inspiration, programming complexity, and educational value**.

Rather than attempting an inaccurate recreation of real aerospace software, the simulator focuses on demonstrating selected mission-control concepts through understandable programming logic.

### Future Improvements

Potential upgrades include:

- Physics-based orbital velocity calculations.
- More realistic fuel and propulsion models.
- Graphical spacecraft telemetry.
- Randomized failure scenarios.
- Advanced fault-recovery algorithms.
- Orbital trajectory visualization.
- Support for different planets and mission types.

**ORBIT-X is a learning platform, not flight-control software.**
